Defect: `buildLifecyclePushData` validates only `kind` and `expiresAtMs`. `alertId` (plain `string`) and `workspaceId` get no shape check at all, despite the doc comment directly above claiming "Every field is validated at this boundary ... an id that could carry text must throw at the call site that introduced it, not hours later from inside a timer" — and `sendLifecycleAlert` (push.ts:2109) repeats the claim verbatim. The opaque-value regex `^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,43}$` runs only in `assertPushDataSafe`, reached via `buildEnvelope` inside `deliver` (push.ts:1425), i.e. per device, inside the enqueued broadcast job.
Failure: A caller passes an alertId that is empty, over 43 chars, or carries text (e.g. a future edit derives it from a branch or workspace name instead of the sha256 in lifecycle-alerts.ts:60). `buildLifecyclePushData` returns it happily; `sendLifecycleAlert` logs `sending lifecycle alert id=<the leaky string>`; the throw finally happens inside `broadcast` -> `deliver` -> `buildEnvelope`, which is wrapped by `enqueue` (push.ts:1546), whose `.catch` swallows it into a single `console.error` and RESOLVES the returned promise. `lifecycle-alerts.send` (lifecycle-alerts.ts:130-146) has already flipped the entry to `state: "sent"` before awaiting, sees no error, and never retries — so the alert is silently never delivered, and the id has already been written to the host log. The one new test (lifecycle-push.test.ts) only exercises the late `assertPushDataSafe` path, so nothing pins the boundary the comment promises. The v1 path does not have this gap in the same way: `arm` calls `buildQuestionPushData(input)` eagerly at push.ts:2043 with the explicit comment "Validate the payload NOW, at the call site that introduced it", and its ids are branded `QuestionId`/`WorkspaceId` rather than a bare `string`.
